research_title = 'Visual Analysis of Prediction Uncertainty in Neural Networks for Deep Image Synthesis'
research_description = 'Co-authored research on understanding prediction uncertainty and sensitivity in neural image synthesis, published in IEEE TVCG.'
research_body = f'''<main id="main" class="container research-detail-page"><section class="case-hero"><a class="back-link" href="../index.html#research">← BACK TO RESEARCH</a><div class="eyebrow">RESEARCH / IEEE TVCG · 2024 · CO-AUTHORED</div><h1>{research_title}</h1><p class="case-lead">How reliable is a neural network’s rendering of scientific data? We compare MC-Dropout and Deep Ensembles to explore uncertainty, error, and sensitivity across viewpoints.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10541088" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the paper ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18545" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open preprint ↗</a></div></section><figure class="research-figure"><div class="case-visual" style="view-transition-name:research-uncertainty">{research_visual()}</div><figcaption>Conceptual illustration of synthesis, uncertainty, and sensitivity—not experimental results. See the paper for measured results and figures.</figcaption></figure><div class="case-layout"><article class="case-content"><h2>The question</h2><p>The model takes camera azimuth and elevation as inputs and predicts a 128 × 128 volume-rendered image. The question is how to inspect its uncertainty, prediction error, and response to small viewpoint changes—not just whether the image looks plausible.</p><h2>My contribution</h2><p>I co-authored this work, examining pixel-level uncertainty, prediction error, and gradient sensitivity using MC-Dropout and Deep Ensembles, alongside interactive visual analysis. The paper reflects a collaborative research effort.</p><h2>The approach</h2><ul><li><strong>Compare two uncertainty methods.</strong> Use 100 stochastic passes through a dropout-enabled model or predictions from 20 separately trained ensemble members. Pixel-wise standard deviation measures uncertainty for each RGB channel.</li><li><strong>Keep the measurements distinct.</strong> Compare predictions with ground truth for pixel-wise error. Compute gradients with respect to the two input view angles to measure viewpoint sensitivity.</li><li><strong>Link overview to detail.</strong> Combine view-space heatmaps, parallel coordinates, lasso selection, and RGB image views so users can compare methods and inspect selected viewpoints.</li></ul><figure class="paper-interface-figure"><a href="../assets/research-interface.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the research interface figure at full size"><img src="../assets/research-interface.png" alt="Figure 5 from the paper: linked parallel coordinates and uncertainty/error heatmaps beside ground-truth, prediction, uncertainty, and error images for Combustion data." width="1450" height="650" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Figure 5 from our research paper: the interactive comparison interface using Combustion data.</figcaption></figure><h2>What the study found</h2><p>Evaluation used Turbulent Combustion, Hurricane Isabel, and Asteroid Impact data, with 10,000 training viewpoints and 512 test viewpoints. Each ensemble member trained on an 80% sample of the training set.</p><ul><li><strong>Image quality versus resource cost.</strong> Ensembles achieved the highest PSNR and lowest MSE on all three datasets. MC-Dropout produced image quality close to the no-dropout baseline while adding uncertainty estimates with much lower training and storage costs than ensembles.</li><li><strong>Uncertainty depends on the view.</strong> Complex structures and boundaries often showed greater uncertainty and error. The interface also exposed differences between RGB channels and viewpoints with high sensitivity.</li><li><strong>More samples had diminishing returns.</strong> Uncertainty and PSNR largely stabilized around 100 MC samples and 15–20 ensemble members in the tested configurations.</li></ul><h2>The trade-off</h2><p>Ensembles were faster at inference in these experiments, despite their higher training and storage costs. MC-Dropout offered a practical single-model alternative with comparable uncertainty patterns. These results describe the tested scientific visualization models and datasets; they are not a universal ranking of the two methods.</p></article><aside class="case-aside" aria-label="Research details"><h2>Publication</h2><p class="research-meta">IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics<br>2024</p><h2>Authors</h2><p class="research-meta">Soumya Dutta<br>Faheem Nizar<br>Ahmad Amaan<br>Ayan Acharya</p><h2>Methods &amp; tools</h2>{tags(research_project)}<h2>Explore</h2><a class="text-link" href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10541088" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Published paper ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18545" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open preprint ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="{research_project['url']}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Code &amp; visualization ↗</a><div class="case-note">Collaborative research. The repository contains the visualization interface and instructions for exploring it locally.</div></aside></div><a class="next-project" href="../index.html#work"><span>EXPLORE MORE →</span><h2>Selected projects</h2></a></main>'''
(ROOT/'projects'/'prediction-uncertainty.html').write_text(document(f'Prediction Uncertainty Research — Ahmad Amaan',research_description,research_body,'../'))
print('Built index.html, five project pages, and the research detail page.')
